     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-11-07 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Delhi Metro Adjusts Diwali Timings Amid Cracker Regulations and AQI Concerns</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Delhi Metro</span> <span>#Diwali</span> <span>#Public Transport</span> <span>#Air Quality</span></div>
       <p>The Delhi Metro Rail Corporation (DMRC) has announced adjusted timings for its services on Sunday, November 8, to accommodate commuters during Diwali festivities. Metro operations on the Pink Line, Magenta Line, and Grey Line will commence an hour earlier than usual, starting at 6 am from their respective terminal stations, rather than the standard Sunday start time of 7 am. This revision aims to facilitate early morning travel for celebrants.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-07/delhi-metro-adjusts-diwali-timings-amid-cracker-regulations-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Specifically, early services on the Pink Line will cover the Majlis Park-Majlis Park/Shiv Vihar stretch. On the Magenta Line, early trains will operate between Botanical Garden-Krishna Park Extension and Majlis Park-Deepali Chowk. The Grey Line's early start will serve the Dwarka-Kakrola-Dhansa Bus Stand section. Following these early commencements, regular train services on these corridors will adhere to the standard Sunday timetable. Additionally, the DMRC confirmed that the last train services from terminal stations across all Delhi Metro lines, including the Airport Express Line, will depart at 10 pm on Sunday.</p><p>Alongside the metro adjustments, Delhi Police have implemented strict regulations regarding firecrackers to mitigate pollution and safeguard sensitive areas. 'No-cracker zones' have been designated, prohibiting the bursting of firecrackers within a 300-meter radius of all hospitals and schools. The Deputy Commissioner of Police (Shahdara) stressed a zero-tolerance policy for violations, with special police teams deployed to monitor compliance and initiate legal action.</p><p>DCP Rajendra Prasad Meena further emphasized that only green crackers, bearing authenticated verification markers and certified QR codes, are permitted for purchase from authorized sources, in accordance with Supreme Court guidelines. The bursting of crackers is restricted to a total window of three hours across two days of the Diwali festival. On both November 7 and 8, crackers may be burst from 6:00 am to 7:00 am, and again from 8:00 pm to 10:00 pm. Violations, whether through bursting or selling illegal crackers, will incur strict legal consequences. Ahead of Diwali, Delhi Police also heightened security and intensified foot patrolling in crowded commercial hubs to maintain law and order and ensure public safety.</p><p>The air quality in Delhi remained a concern leading up to Diwali. On Saturday morning, the day before the festival, the Air Quality Index (AQI) was recorded at 364, categorizing it as 'very poor'. This followed a 24-hour average AQI of 382 at 4 pm on Friday, according to data from the Central Board of Pollution Control (CPCB). Out of 59 air quality monitoring stations in the city, 18 reported AQI levels in the 'severe' category on Saturday morning, with forecasts from the Air Quality Early Warning System for Delhi anticipating the AQI to reach 'severe' on Saturday.</p><p>What to watch: The effectiveness of these measures on Diwali day traffic flow and air quality.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately reflects the provided full text, including specific metro routes, police regulations, and air quality data points.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/transportation/railways/diwali-2026-delhi-metro-to-commence-services-at-6-am-on-select-routes-check-new-timings-here/articleshow/135626954.cms" rel="noopener">Economic Times</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       <div class="cityguide"><b>City guide</b><p>More on <a href="https://bharatdn.com/city/delhi/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Delhi</a> - census 2011 population, live weather and air quality, local food and getaways.</p></div>
