     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-11-07 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge conf" title="Reported by Crictracker, ESPN Cricinfo">confirmed by 2 sources</span></div>
       <h1>Hardik Pandya - Arshdeep Singh Trade Nears Completion, Mumbai Indians Set for New Captain in IPL 2027</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#IPL</span> <span>#Trade</span> <span>#Hardik Pandya</span></div>
       <p>A one-for-one player exchange between Punjab Kings and Mumbai Indians is on the verge of being formalised, with Hardik Pandya heading to Mohali and Arshdeep Singh making the journey in the opposite direction. Both franchises have submitted their Expressions of Interest (EOI) under the BCCI-prescribed trade framework, and both players have provided written consent to the arrangement, with no third player featuring in the deal. BCCI ratification remains the sole procedural step, though official holidays in India have contributed to a delay. The Board is expected to clear the paperwork shortly, after which the trade will be formally recognised and both Pandya and Arshdeep will be registered under their new franchises for IPL 2027. According to ESPN Cricinfo, once the trade goes through, Mumbai Indians will have a new captain for IPL 2027.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-07/hardik-pandya-arshdeep-singh-trade-nears-completion-mumbai-i.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The Board's trade regulations explicitly state, &quot;Only players whose names are included on an EOI Confirmation and for whom a signed Consent Form has been received by the BCCI may be traded,&quot; as quoted by Cricbuzz. Mumbai Indians identified Arshdeep Singh as the missing piece in their pace attack, following Deepak Chahar's underwhelming campaigns in recent IPL seasons. Arshdeep is widely regarded as India's leading new-ball operator in white-ball cricket, and his capacity to bowl effectively at the death offers value across all phases of an innings. With Jasprit Bumrah as their foremost death-overs specialist and Trent Boult providing left-arm swing, adding Arshdeep would give Mumbai Indians a three-pronged seam unit capable of threatening batters at every stage of a match. Arshdeep is Punjab Kings' all-time highest wicket-taker in IPL history, and the franchise had retained him at the IPL 2025 auction for ₹18 crore, exercising their Right to Match card, making his departure a significant shift.</p><p>For Hardik Pandya, the move to Punjab Kings adds another chapter to a career marked by franchise changes. After three years at Gujarat Titans, where he captained the side, he returned to Mumbai Indians ahead of the 2024 season and took charge of the captaincy from Rohit Sharma. That stint at Mumbai Indians now appears to be concluding. At Punjab Kings, Pandya arrives into a squad that has grown considerably in stature, with Shreyas Iyer establishing himself as the leader over the past two seasons. Under Iyer's captaincy, Punjab Kings reached their first IPL final since 2014 during the 2025 season. Pandya's all-round ability is expected to deepen that squad further, and his experience of earning the India captaincy underlines the calibre of cricketer the franchise is set to welcome. The financial terms of the trade have not been disclosed. Jasprit Bumrah remains central to Mumbai Indians' plans, and his partnership with the incoming Arshdeep Singh will be one of the more closely watched bowling combinations heading into IPL 2027.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft comprehensively incorporates all available facts from the source text and provides necessary context regarding the trade.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>How this was reported · 2 sources</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.crictracker.com/cricket-news/hardik-pandya-arshdeep-singh-trade-nears-completion-7219/" rel="noopener">Crictracker</a></li><li><a href="https://www.cricinfo.com/ci/content/story/1561288.html?ex_cid=OTC-RSS" rel="noopener">ESPN Cricinfo</a></li></ul>
      <p>Synthesised from 2 independent reports on the same event. Details are attributed to the outlet that carried them; nothing outside these sources is published.</p>
    </div>
