     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · Times of India · 2026-11-07 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>IEA Warns of Global Gas Crunch Amid Strait of Hormuz Disruptions</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The International Energy Agency (IEA) projects a global LNG supply decline of 10 to 25 billion cubic meters (bcm) for the 2026/27 winter. This shortfall stems from an unprecedented supply shock caused by restricted shipments through the Strait of Hormuz, where loadings from Qatar and the UAE dropped by 60 bcm between March and October 2026.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-07/iea-warns-of-global-gas-crunch-amid-strait-of-hormuz-disrupt.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>While new projects in North America, Australia, and West Africa are expected to contribute approximately 20 bcm of incremental supply, this growth remains insufficient to offset Persian Gulf losses. With limited long-distance pipeline capacity, the IEA warns that tighter market conditions will persist throughout the northern hemisphere winter.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the complex IEA report, including the specific bcm figures and the factors involved in the winter supply outlook.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/business/international-business/why-new-lng-projects-may-not-prevent-a-global-gas-crunch-this-winter/articleshow/135627696.cms" rel="noopener">Times of India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
