     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-11-07 · editor 8/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New 'High Safety' Plasticizer Linked to Lower Birth Weight</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Environment</span> <span>#Chemicals</span> <span>#Plasticizers</span> <span>#Dinch</span></div>
       <p>A new peer-reviewed study has linked exposure to Dinch, a new generation of plasticizers, to lower birth weight and fetal development complications. These complications could potentially lead to serious health problems later in life. Dinch was introduced by the industry as a “high safety” and nontoxic alternative to common phthalate plasticizers, whose health risks became evident over recent decades.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-07/new-high-safety-plasticizer-linked-to-lower-birth-weight.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Despite its claims of safety, Dinch is now widely used in various consumer products. It is found in soft plastic food packaging across the United States and the European Union. Beyond food packaging, the chemical is also present in children’s toys, medical devices, certain textiles, flooring, and other consumer goods, indicating broad exposure potential.</p><p>The study’s findings raise significant concerns regarding the safety of chemicals currently deemed acceptable for widespread use. The observed links between Dinch exposure and adverse fetal development outcomes highlight the need for ongoing scrutiny of new industrial chemicals, even those marketed as safer alternatives.</p><p>What to watch: Further research and potential regulatory responses to Dinch's widespread use.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately reflects the source summary provided, maintaining a neutral tone and covering the key claims regarding Dinch plasticizers.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/nov/07/nontoxic-plastic-lower-birth-weight-study" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-11-07/new-high-safety-plasticizer-linked-to-lower-birth-weight" data-title="New 'High Safety' Plasticizer Linked to Lower Birth Weight"><b>Share:</b>
