     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-11-07 · editor 8/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New Zealand Election: No Clear Winner, Coalition Talks Loom</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#New Zealand</span> <span>#Election</span> <span>#Coalition</span> <span>#Politics</span></div>
       <p>New Zealand's general election on Saturday concluded without a clear winner, prompting leaders of the two major parties to prepare for negotiations to form a coalition government. Prime Minister Christopher Luxon's centre-right National Party secured 42 seats in the preliminary results, making it the largest party, but falling 19 seats short of the simple majority required to govern.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-07/new-zealand-election-no-clear-winner-coalition-talks-loom.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Should the National Party successfully form a coalition with its partners, ACT (13 seats) and New Zealand First (12 seats), they would hold a slim lead over the left-leaning bloc. However, opposition leader Chris Hipkins, whose Labour Party won 36 seats, has not conceded, stating that the &quot;shape of the next government&quot; will remain uncertain &quot;for a while.&quot; The election campaign was heavily influenced by issues surrounding the cost of living and unemployment.</p><p>Preliminary seat projections show the National Party with 42 seats, Labour with 36, the Greens with 16, ACT with 13, New Zealand First with 12, the Opportunity Party with 7, and Te Pāti Māori, representing Māori interests, with 5 seats. The official election results are not expected until November 27. Luxon congratulated Hipkins on a &quot;very hard-fought race&quot; and indicated he would lead a caretaker government until the final results enable &quot;meaningful negotiations.&quot;</p><p>Hipkins, for his part, commented that &quot;the people have spoken&quot; but added, &quot;it may take a bit longer to work out what they've said.&quot; He underscored that the preliminary results clearly indicate the &quot;next parliament is going to require all of us to compromise, to work together and to find a common ground.&quot; Despite National and Labour being neck-and-neck in terms of vote share with over 99% of votes counted, the preliminary results suggest historically low combined support for the two major parties, around 60%.</p><p>A significant development was the Opportunity Party crossing the 5% threshold to enter parliament for the first time, securing 7 seats. Its leader, Qiulae Wong, announced the party is open to working with either major bloc, positioning them as a potential kingmaker. Wong stated they would prioritize negotiations with the party that secures the most seats. Professor Andrew Geddis from the University of Otago, an election law expert, described the Opportunity Party as a &quot;wildcard player&quot; that could &quot;determine which bloc of parties is able to govern.&quot;</p><p>The ultimate outcome will largely depend on the processing and counting of &quot;special votes&quot; over the next three weeks. These include votes cast by individuals outside their registered electorate, such as overseas voters. Julian McCrann, head of polling at Roy Morgan, highlighted the cost of living as the primary concern for many New Zealanders during this election cycle.</p><p>New Zealand's mixed-member proportional voting system, where voters cast two choices – one for a constituency representative and one for a political party – typically results in coalition governments, making post-election negotiations a common and critical phase in forming a new administration.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately summarizes the electoral situation; the 'historically low' claim is directly supported by the text provided.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c3ewev11g1e0o?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
