     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-11-07 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Oil Marketing Companies Dispute Karnataka's E20 Ethanol Blend Report</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>State-owned oil marketing companies (OMCs) have rejected Karnataka's findings that petrol contained up to 31% ethanol, arguing the state's testing failed to follow statutory procedures. While the OMCs' own tests of 56 samples averaged 19.3% ethanol, they asserted that the government's use of gas chromatography lacked necessary validation against IS 17021 standards.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-07/oil-marketing-companies-dispute-karnatakas-e20-ethanol-blend.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The OMCs have requested complete documentation, outlet-wise reports, and access to samples for retesting. They highlighted a density discrepancy in the state's data, noting that higher ethanol concentrations should increase fuel density, and reaffirmed their commitment to joint testing under the Marketing Discipline Guidelines.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Provides a comprehensive summary of the OMCs' technical and procedural objections.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/business/Industry/omcs-refute-karnatakas-e20-report-seeks-additional-details-about-procedure/article71675835.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
