     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-07 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Pakistani Astronaut to Make History on China's Space Station</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Science</span> <span>#Space Exploration</span> <span>#China</span> <span>#Pakistan</span></div>
       <p>A Pakistani astronaut is set to join the crew of China's Shenzhou 24 spacecraft, a mission poised to mark a historic first: the boarding of Beijing's space station by a foreign national. This significant development was reported by state media on Saturday, November 7, 2026. According to the China Manned Space Agency, the astronaut will complete a short on-orbit flight and then return to Earth aboard the Shenzhou 23 spacecraft. China's official news agency Xinhua conveyed these details, though a specific launch date for the mission has not yet been announced.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-07/pakistani-astronaut-to-make-history-on-chinas-space-station.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This impending mission represents a pivotal moment, as it will be the inaugural joint flight involving both Chinese and foreign astronauts in the entire history of China's manned space program. The groundwork for this collaborative endeavor was established earlier in 2026. Xinhua previously reported that two Pakistani candidates, Muhammad Zeeshan Ali and Khurram Daud, had commenced comprehensive training. They have been preparing alongside their Chinese counterparts, with the explicit plan for one of them to be ultimately selected as a payload specialist for a short-term spaceflight.</p><p>The rigorous training program, as detailed by Xinhua, encompassed not only the development of essential hands-on operational capabilities but also included intensive Chinese language instruction. This linguistic component is deemed vital for ensuring seamless integration with the Chinese crew and operational systems. This upcoming flight directly implements a significant space cooperation agreement that was signed between China and Pakistan in February 2025, solidifying a deepening partnership in space exploration efforts.</p><p>Beyond this specific mission, China is concurrently making substantial advancements in its broader space agenda. The nation is actively preparing for its first crewed lunar landing, an ambitious goal targeted by 2030. This pursuit places China in direct competition with the United States, its perceived top space rival, as NASA aims to land its astronauts on the lunar surface two years earlier, in 2028. The inclusion of a foreign national on China's space station underscores the nation's expanding international engagement and its strategic approach to global space endeavors.</p><p>What to watch: The announcement of the selected Pakistani astronaut and the precise launch schedule for the Shenzhou 24 mission.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of the source material including names, dates, and future context.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-pakistani-astronaut-board-shenzhou-foreigner.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
