     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-07 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>SEBI Implements New Settlement Rules: What it Means for Applicants</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#SEBI</span> <span>#Financial Regulation</span> <span>#Settlement Rules</span> <span>#Investor Protection</span></div>
       <p>The Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI) introduced its revised settlement regulations on November 7, 2026, offering individuals and entities a new framework to resolve administrative and civil proceedings. These &quot;SEBI (Settlement of Administrative and Civil Proceedings) Regulations, 2026,&quot; which supersede the 2018 rules, modify application fees, minimum settlement amounts, and application timelines. For ordinary savers or taxpayers who might face regulatory issues, these changes outline the financial and procedural aspects of seeking a settlement, including new fast-track options.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-07/sebi-implements-new-settlement-rules-what-it-means-for-appli.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Under the new rules, a non-refundable application fee of ₹25,000 is required for natural persons, while other applicants face a fee of ₹35,000, separate from the actual settlement amount. SEBI has established minimum settlement amounts, with the final figure calculated using a formula (Regulation 10) that factors in a base amount, the stage of proceedings, regulatory action, gravity of the violation, and aggravating or mitigating circumstances. Crucially, the settlement amount cannot fall below ₹3 lakh or ₹7 lakh, depending on the case.</p><p>Applicants generally have 90 days from receiving a show-cause notice to file for settlement under Regulation 4. For proceedings already pending before SEBI as of November 7, 2026, a transitional provision allows eligible applicants—including those whose previous applications were rejected or withdrawn under the 2018 rules—to apply by February 4, 2027. However, these transitional applications incur a 20% higher settlement amount. A new settlement notice mechanism also provides a 60-day window to apply before a show-cause notice is even issued, though this is not available for cases involving interim directions or prosecution.</p><p>The revised framework introduces two fast-track settlement mechanisms. The &quot;violation-based&quot; option applies to specific violations like delayed disclosures, offering a 30-day application window, extendable by 15 days. The &quot;monetary threshold-based&quot; mechanism applies when the settlement amount is up to ₹10 lakh and does not require disgorgement or remedial terms, processing such cases directly without referral to the High Powered Advisory Committee. Certain cases, such as those involving wilful defaulters, fraudulent borrowers, or fugitive economic offenders, remain ineligible for settlement. Additionally, proceedings with market-wide impact, large-scale investor losses, or adverse effects on market integrity may not be settled unless these concerns can be adequately addressed.</p><p>To assist applicants, SEBI has launched an online settlement application facility on its website, along with a calculator to estimate settlement amounts, FAQs, and an explanatory video. While these tools aim to streamline the process, filing an application does not guarantee acceptance, as all applications undergo examination by an Internal Committee and, where applicable, a High Powered Advisory Committee, before a decision by the Panel of Whole Time Members. It is important to note that SEBI can still issue interim directions to protect investors and market integrity even if a settlement application is pending.</p><p>What to watch: The impact of these streamlined processes on the resolution of regulatory proceedings.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Excellent synthesis of SEBI's new settlement rules, capturing fees, deadlines, fast-track options, and exclusions.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/sebi-settlement-rules-from-november-7-who-can-apply-online-how-much-to-pay-and-the-90-day-deadline-explained-11794046408426.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
