     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-11-07 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Ukraine's Energy Infrastructure Hit as Russian Strikes Escalate Ahead of Talks</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#World News</span> <span>#Ukraine</span> <span>#Russia</span> <span>#Conflict</span></div>
       <p>Overnight drone and ballistic missile attacks have severely damaged Ukraine's energy infrastructure, President Volodymyr Zelensky announced on November 7, 2026. These strikes caused widespread blackouts and disrupted water supplies across the country, with Zelensky stating that 12 Ukrainian regions came under attack, and the capital Kyiv being the hardest hit. The wave of aggression involved more than 30 ballistic missiles and over 170 drones, resulting in nine reported injuries, including one child, across Ukraine.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-07/ukraines-energy-infrastructure-hit-as-russian-strikes-escala.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>President Zelensky, writing on Telegram, directly linked the attacks to upcoming trilateral talks scheduled for next week in Abu Dhabi, where Ukrainian and Russian delegations are due to participate. He accused Moscow of launching these assaults on Ukraine's &quot;critical infrastructure&quot; as a preparatory measure for the discussions. The timing is particularly critical as temperatures are dropping in Ukraine, with parts of the country already experiencing the first winter snows, exacerbating the impact of utility disruptions.</p><p>In Kyiv, the situation remains challenging, with President Zelensky confirming disruptions to electricity, water, and heating supplies. Residential buildings, the North Bridge, and a kindergarten suffered damage. In the wider Kyiv region, substations connecting the Rivne and Khmelnytsky nuclear power plants to Kyiv and central Ukraine were struck. Despite the challenges, Kyiv's Mayor Vitali Klitschko reported that the city's water supply system has returned to normal operation, and approximately 60% of the city's apartment buildings are now connected to the heating system.</p><p>Energy minister Denys Shmyhal noted power cuts in five regions, though the exact number of affected homes was not specified. Beyond Kyiv, substations were also damaged in the Vinnytsia, Zhytomyr, Rivne, Kharkiv, and Dnipro regions, as stated by President Zelensky. These latest strikes follow a diplomatic effort earlier in the week, where US First Lady Melania Trump hosted Russian and Ukrainian delegations at the White House on Thursday, calling for a &quot;more enduring&quot; ceasefire.</p><p>Last week, a six-day ceasefire in the southern Kherson region saw hundreds of residents evacuate the Russian-occupied town of Oleshky. Both Moscow and Kyiv credited Melania Trump with brokering this truce. &quot;The lives of innocent people must transcend the division of war. I hope Oleshky inspires greater peace,&quot; Trump remarked. However, the recent attacks underscore persistent tensions. Earlier this week, a separate incident near Zaporizhzhia involved glide bombs hitting an aid centre, with the death toll rising to 55 as search teams continue their work in the ruins.</p><p>In his Saturday Telegram post, Zelensky reiterated Ukraine's reliance on increased bilateral support from the United States. He emphasized, &quot;We are counting on unity between both parties on issues of security, support for Ukraine, condemnation of Russian aggression, and increased sanctions pressure on Putin.&quot; He further added that &quot;The resolve and consistency of the United States in supporting Ukraine are of great importance in bringing peace closer.&quot; Meanwhile, Zelensky's spokesperson declined to comment on reports regarding a potential invitation for the Ukrainian president to visit the UK for a state visit next year.</p><p>What to watch: The outcome of the trilateral talks in Abu Dhabi and further developments in international diplomatic efforts.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Thorough reporting that fully captures all facts, figures, and details present in the BBC source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
