     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-07 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Voluntary Discharge Forfeits Pension Rights, Meghalaya HC Rules</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Pension</span> <span>#Meghalaya High Court</span> <span>#Government Service</span> <span>#Financial Planning</span></div>
       <p>The Meghalaya High Court has dismissed a pension claim by a former Assam Rifles Rifleman, L. Pearlson, who sought retirement benefits after serving for over 13 years. The court ruled that a voluntary discharge from service, even if requested by the employee, constitutes resignation and leads to the forfeiture of past service under the Central Civil Services (Pension) Rules, 1972. This clarifies that merely completing 10 years of qualifying service under Rule 49(2) does not guarantee pensionary benefits if Rule 26(1), which governs forfeiture upon resignation, applies.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-07/voluntary-discharge-forfeits-pension-rights-meghalaya-hc-rul.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Pearlson, appointed as a Rifleman on January 3, 1986, was discharged on June 30, 1999. He alleged his signatures were obtained on a pre-typed document, implying his discharge was not voluntary. However, the respondents disputed this, asserting that Pearlson had applied for premature discharge in 1998 on &quot;extreme compassionate grounds&quot; due to his wife's illness. They maintained that he was aware that accepting discharge on his own request would render him ineligible for pensionary benefits.</p><p>The High Court examined the discharge certificate and another document dated December 23, 1998. These records established that Pearlson had indeed sought discharge on compassionate grounds and was aware of his ineligibility for pensionary benefits. Furthermore, the court rejected Pearlson’s challenge on the grounds of &quot;delay and laches,&quot; noting that he approached the authorities in 2021, nearly 22 years after his discharge, without providing a convincing explanation for the substantial delay.</p><p>In its ruling, the court explicitly stated, &quot;Voluntarily resignation from a post entails forfeiture of all past services and is therefore not entitled to pensionary benefits.&quot; It clarified that Rule 26(1) acts as a bar to future service benefits, making Rule 49(2) inapplicable in such scenarios. Therefore, Pearlson’s claim for pension could not be accepted simply because he had served for more than 13 years.</p><p>For ordinary savers and taxpayers, this ruling highlights a crucial distinction in employment terms, particularly for government service. It underscores that while a certain period of service might typically qualify one for a pension, the *manner* of separation is equally critical. Voluntary resignation, as interpreted by this court, can negate years of service for pension purposes. Employees contemplating leaving service prematurely should always scrutinize their specific service rules to understand the full implications for their long-term financial planning and pension eligibility.</p><p>Despite dismissing the pension petition, the Meghalaya High Court directed the authorities to release ₹5,656 towards Pearlson's Individual Running Ledger Account (IRLA) and ₹53,642 towards his General Provident Fund (GPF) account. These payments are to be made within 30 days if they have not been released already.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Excellent summary of the legal ruling, the specific rules cited, and the court-ordered payouts.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/meghalaya-hc-denies-pension-to-ex-assam-rifles-rifleman-says-voluntary-discharge-leads-to-forfeiture-of-past-service-11794052829928.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
